From fdbe4b619926a53b0e0c5d031fc9bc0ec4931291 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: orthogonaleety <108008609+orthogonaleety@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:13:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Install warning when sysconfig conflicts with linux-surface
 as default

There exists machinery in standard (grub) Fedora builds, which
upon install of every kernel update selects such as the default.
This is configurable to some specifically named kernel package.
Typically this results in such as the standard Fedora kernel
fighting and wresting selection back from linux-surface,
and winning even against the linux-surface watchdog.
What is more, this machinery can be leveraged by the user as a
proper means of selecting our kernel as the default after install.

This change helps in this area by checking this standard
configuration at kernel install time, and just warning the user,
in the dnf terminal output and also in the journal, if the current
config might fight our kernel and could be amended to help.

It is interested first only in whether some kernel other than
our package is being targeted.
Provided our kernel package has been configured for targeting,
if it then finds that acting upon new kernels is not enabled,
it warns about such.
The script does not effect changes to these key admin files/choices.

This patches kernel.spec.template, from which rpmbuild assembles
the packages' %post scripts.
Text *following* the stock kernel's %kernel_variant_post call
is assembled into the end of kernel-core's %post.

The linux-surface build scripts effect with_stock and by
spec's default with_base.
So %{with_stock_base} is the block we place our check and warning in.

(Tested and evaluated by the author,) but 'heavily' and for
flow location especially, co-authored-by: ...

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 redhat/kernel.spec.template | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+)

diff --git a/redhat/kernel.spec.template b/redhat/kernel.spec.template
index 29dce19ad93..a7d4b3db562 100644
--- a/redhat/kernel.spec.template
+++ b/redhat/kernel.spec.template
@@ -4520,6 +4520,31 @@ fi\
 %if %{with_stock_base}
 %kernel_variant_preun -e
 %kernel_variant_post
+# Warn (stderr and journal) if DEFAULTKERNEL names some other kernel package,
+# or if it names this one but UPDATEDEFAULT is not "yes".
+# Check only: the file is never modified, and any problem reading it is silent.
+if [ -r /etc/sysconfig/kernel ]; then
+  (
+    . /etc/sysconfig/kernel 2>/dev/null || exit 0
+    warn() {
+      printf '%s\n%s\n' "$1" "$2" >&2
+      logger -t %{name} -p user.warning -- "$1 ${2#\* }" 2>/dev/null || :
+    }
+    pkg=%{name}
+#   Matches not PACKAGENAME, kernel-PACKAGENAME , kernel-PACKAGENAME-core
+    if [ -n "$DEFAULTKERNEL" ] && [ "$DEFAULTKERNEL" != "$pkg" ] && [ "$DEFAULTKERNEL" != "$pkg-core" ] &&
+       [ "$DEFAULTKERNEL" != "${pkg#kernel-}" ]; then
+      warn "warning: default kernel may not be set to %{name} following this install." \
+           "* Consult documentation for your system on selecting the default kernel."
+      warn "warning: %{name} may lose default status to other kernel packages, such as the standard Fedora kernel." \
+           "* Consider amending /etc/sysconfig/kernel to: DEFAULTKERNEL=%{name}"
+#   Targeting our package, so warn if not updating as default
+    elif [ -n "$DEFAULTKERNEL" ] && [ "$UPDATEDEFAULT" != "yes" ]; then
+      warn "warning: default kernel may not be set to %{name} following this install." \
+           "* Consider amending /etc/sysconfig/kernel to: UPDATEDEFAULT=yes"
+    fi
+  ) || :
+fi
 %endif
 
 %if %{with_zfcpdump}
-- 
